Again there is an exercise here to show $\sigma x \in |B|$. In particular $\sigma\perp \in |B|$ for all $\sigma \in \Sigma^*$, and these are just the finite elements. The minimal element of $\sigma\perp$ is $\sigma\Delta$. Note that $\sigma_0\perp \subseteq \sigma_1\perp$ if and only if $\sigma_0$ is an *initial segment* of the sequence $\sigma_1$.

<!-- formalization -->

**Theorem (`prepend_append`).** Prepending concatenates: $\sigma(\tau X)=(\sigma\tau)X$.

*Proof.* Expand both sides as concatenations of strings. ∎

**Theorem (`prepend_mono`).** Prepending preserves inclusion: $X'\subseteq X$ implies $\sigma X'\subseteq\sigma X$.

*Proof.* Every $\sigma\tau$ with $\tau\in X'$ is likewise in $\sigma X$. ∎

**Theorem (`prepend_univ`).** Prepending the master set yields the cone: $\sigma\Delta=\sigma\Sigma^*$.

*Proof.* Extensions of $\sigma$ are exactly concatenations $\sigma\tau$ for $\tau\in\Sigma^*$. ∎

**Theorem (`sigmaBot_le_iff`).** One has $\sigma_0\perp\le\sigma_1\perp$ if and only if $\sigma_0$ is a prefix of $\sigma_1$.

*Proof.* Principals compare by reverse inclusion of cones, and cones reverse-include exactly when the shorter string prefixes the longer. ∎

**Theorem (`sigmaElt_append`).** Prepending concatenates on elements: $(\sigma\tau)x=\sigma(\tau x)$.

*Proof.* Unwind the definition of $\sigma x$ and use that prepending concatenates on sets. ∎

**Theorem (`sigmaElt_bot`).** Prepending bottom yields the principal: $\sigma\perp=\sigma\cdot\perp$.

*Proof.* The only neighbourhood of $\perp$ is $\Delta$, and $\sigma\Delta$ is the cone of $\sigma$. ∎

**Theorem (`sigmaElt_nil`).** The empty string acts as identity: $\Lambda x=x$.

*Proof.* Prepending the empty string leaves sets unchanged. ∎
